<h2>How to buy tickets online: the seven steps</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Find your event.</strong> Search by performer, team, city or category. Our <a href="/buy-tickets-online">buy tickets online page</a> is a good starting point, and you can also browse <a href="/concert-tickets-for-sale">concert tickets</a>, <a href="/game-day-tickets">game day tickets</a> or <a href="/buy-broadway-tickets">Broadway tickets</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Check the date, time and venue.</strong> Confirm all three on the event page before you go further. Similar names and repeat dates are easy to mix up.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose how many tickets and which seats.</strong> Use the filters to narrow by price or section, and compare a few options.</li>
  <li><strong>Review the total.</strong> Ticket prices and any fees, such as service or delivery fees and taxes, are shown during the purchase process. Make sure the total matches what you expect.</li>
  <li><strong>Enter your details and pay.</strong> Use accurate contact information so your confirmation and tickets reach you.</li>
  <li><strong>Save your confirmation.</strong> Order confirmations are sent by email after successful payment. Keep your order ID.</li>
  <li><strong>Get your tickets.</strong> Access them through your confirmation email or your account dashboard.</li>
</ol>

<h3>How to buy tickets online: comparing seats before you pay</h3>
<p>Two seats with the same label can feel very different. When you learn how to buy tickets online, learn to read the seat information too. Look at the section and row, note whether the event has assigned seating or general admission, and think about what matters most: a close view, an aisle or a lower total price.</p>
<p>Sports events usually have assigned seating, and theater shows often limit late entry, so read the event details. If you are weighing cost, our <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals page</a> and <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo codes page</a> are worth a look, although availability changes.</p>

